WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.213
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:06.000
SPEAKER: Workload
Identity allows

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:08.070
workloads in your Google
Kubernetes Engine,

00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:12.090
or GKE, clusters to impersonate
Identity and Access Management,

00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:16.320
or IAM, service accounts so that
they can access Google Cloud

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:17.010
Services.

00:00:17.010 --> 00:00:19.050
Without Workload
Identity enabled,

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:23.310
your workloads will use your GKE
nodes default service account.

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:25.080
In this video, we
will show you how

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:27.990
to enable Workload Identity
on your GKE cluster

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:30.000
and configure your
apps to use it.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.280
You can enable
Workload Identity using

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:37.240
the Cloud Console, the Google
Cloud CLI, or the REST API.

00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:38.830
We'll first use
the Cloud Console

00:00:38.830 --> 00:00:41.920
to enable Workload Identity
on an existing cluster,

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:44.410
and then we'll use the
CLI to configure an app

00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:45.790
to use Workload Identity.

00:00:45.790 --> 00:00:47.540
Let's get started.

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:49.120
Go to the Kubernetes
Engine page.

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:55.190
Select your cluster.

00:00:55.190 --> 00:00:56.840
Scroll down to the
Security section.

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:01.730
And under this section,
click the Edit icon

00:01:01.730 --> 00:01:03.860
next to Workload Identity.

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:06.080
Click the box to enable
Workload Identity,

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:07.520
then save your changes.

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:09.170
After a few minutes,
your cluster

00:01:09.170 --> 00:01:11.750
is enabled with
Workload Identity.

00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:12.740
Congratulations.

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:15.950
You now have a Workload
Identity-enabled cluster.

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:18.740
The node pool that we used
already had a test pod running.

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:21.260
Let's see what service
account it uses.

00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:23.930
First, we'll connect
to the cluster.

00:01:23.930 --> 00:01:24.500
Great.

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:25.580
We're in.

00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:27.005
Let's exec into the test pod.

00:01:29.810 --> 00:01:32.960
Then, run the following
command, gcloud auth list,

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:35.660
to find out which Google
service account is used.

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:38.930
It shows our nodes
default service account.

00:01:38.930 --> 00:01:42.440
Let's configure our pod to
use Workload Identity instead.

00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:45.590
Now, we'll use the Google Cloud
CLI to tell an existing node

00:01:45.590 --> 00:01:48.200
pool to use Workload Identity.

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:50.780
We'll exit our test pod,
then update the node pool

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:54.530
to use the GKE metadata server.

00:01:54.530 --> 00:01:56.190
This will take a few minutes.

00:01:56.190 --> 00:01:59.320
You can also create
a new node pool.

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:01.660
When your cluster has
Workload Identity enabled,

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:05.500
any new node pools will, too.

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:07.510
Next, create an
IAM service account

00:02:07.510 --> 00:02:09.970
for our pod to impersonate.

00:02:09.970 --> 00:02:12.580
Now, we need to bind the
Kubernetes service account

00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:14.853
that our pod uses to
the IAM service account

00:02:14.853 --> 00:02:15.520
that we created.

00:02:18.630 --> 00:02:20.130
Allow the Kubernetes
service account

00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:21.900
to impersonate the
Google service account

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:24.870
by adding an IAM policy
binding between the two service

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:27.560
accounts.

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:30.170
In our case, we're using the
default Kubernetes service

00:02:30.170 --> 00:02:34.240
account in the
default namespace.

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:37.078
Next, annotate the
Kubernetes service account

00:02:37.078 --> 00:02:38.620
so that Workload
Identity knows which

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:40.870
Google service account to use.

00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:42.670
Workload Identity
is now configured

00:02:42.670 --> 00:02:45.600
with the specified
Google service account.

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:46.230
Great.

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:48.150
We have successfully
enabled and configured

00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:50.850
Workload Identity
on our GKE cluster.

00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:52.560
Time to test it out.

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:55.290
Remember, the test pod was
using the default node service

00:02:55.290 --> 00:02:56.070
account earlier.

00:02:56.070 --> 00:02:59.770
Let's see which service
account is being used now.

00:02:59.770 --> 00:03:06.290
Let's exec back into the test
pod and run gcloud auth list.

00:03:06.290 --> 00:03:08.030
It now shows the
Google service account

00:03:08.030 --> 00:03:09.560
we just created and configured.

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:12.110
That's because we bound our
default Kubernetes service

00:03:12.110 --> 00:03:14.550
account to the IAM
service account,

00:03:14.550 --> 00:03:16.820
so the pod was able to
impersonate the IAM service

00:03:16.820 --> 00:03:18.600
account.

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:19.620
Congratulations.

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:22.680
You now have a Workload
Identity-enabled GKE cluster

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:25.890
configured to use an IAM service
account with your workload.

00:03:25.890 --> 00:03:28.770
Read more about Workload
Identity in the documentation.

00:03:28.770 --> 00:03:32.120
[MUSIC PLAYING]

